<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ruvuma River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rovuma River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ruvuma, called the Rovuma in Portuguese, is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, about 800 km long, that rises in southern Tanzania and flows east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. For most of its course it forms the border between Tanzania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, agreed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and Portugal in 1886. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a> explored its lower course in 1861–1862.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ruvuma-river.jpg" alt="Ruvuma River" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Livingstone · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Near_the_Cataracts_of_the_Rovuma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the headquarters of Portuguese power in East Africa, its capture by Omani forces in 1698 after a long siege ended Portuguese rule north of the <b>Ruvuma</b>, and the town later became the base of the Mazrui governors and, under British rule, the starting point of the Uganda Railway.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mombasa">Mombasa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Lacking the money to occupy northern Mozambique, Portugal granted a charter in 1891 to the Companhia do Niassa, covering the land between the <b>Rovuma</b> and Lúrio rivers from the coast to Lake Malawi.</blockquote><a href="/state/niassa-company">Niassa Company</a></li></ul></section>
